Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary American drama; naturalistic faces, weathered Milwaukee interiors, and grounded Pacific Northwest realism.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A worn fifth-floor Milwaukee apartment. MA, OLIVIA OLSEN, a tired older woman with thinning brown hair and a cardigan, watches a game show beneath a faded afghan. Her younger son, lean dark-haired NARRATOR, brings groceries inside.

(SOUND: game-show applause, elevator cables groaning beyond the hall door)

NARRATOR V.O.
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

EXT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

NICK OLSEN, broad-shouldered, scarred over his left eyebrow and chin, with unruly reddish-brown hair, carries six grocery bags as if they weigh nothing. Gym members move aside for him.

(SOUND: barbells clanging, a heavy bag thudding)

NARRATOR V.O.
His face looked like a puzzle held together with glue. He wasn’t ugly. He had just had a few brawls.

NICK

You hold the rest, Bro.

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick always said he would stand up for me. I believed him.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Twelve-year-old NICK stands before furious MRS. ENGEL at her shattered kitchen window. Young NARRATOR and MARK CONNER watch beside dented garbage-can-lid bases.

(SOUND: loose glass tinkling onto linoleum, children’s kickball bouncing)

MRS. ENGEL

Who broke my window?

NICK

I did.

NARRATOR V.O.
Mark kicked it through the window. Nick took it, and a week later gave Ma the cash.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Adult NARRATOR finds MA at the kitchen counter, staring at pill bottles arranged in a line. Her hands tremble over them.

(SOUND: refrigerator hum, pill bottles clicking softly)

MA

Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR

Ma, your pills are right in front of you.

MA

Oh. Right. I don’t know what got into me.

NARRATOR V.O.
Ma had worked herself down to nothing. Somebody had to watch her.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

NARRATOR parks his bicycle outside a modest pizza shop. SIONNA MCALLISTER, pale and slender with short brown hair, luminous light-blue eyes, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue tennis shoes, and a fish-symbol necklace, trips into him.

(SOUND: bicycle chain ticking, traffic hissing over wet pavement)

NARRATOR

Are you okay?

SIONNA

Yes.

NARRATOR

I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA

Sionna.

NARRATOR

Sionna? That’s different.

SIONNA

My parents named me for an Irish goddess. The River Shannon.

NARRATOR V.O.
Her eyes were like the sky before sunrise.

NARRATOR

Want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA

No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College students fill a coffee shop with books and backpacks. SIONNA sits at a small rear table in a loose green hooded top, her blue eyes fixed on NARRATOR as he opens a library copy of Wuthering Heights.

(SOUND: espresso machine steaming, cups rattling in saucers)

SIONNA

What are you reading?

NARRATOR

Wuthering Heights. It’s about a guy and girl who grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA

The moors are like the people. Wild, unpredictable, infertile.

SIONNA

Heathcliff’s love never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR V.O.
Something inside of me was coming awake.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

NICK, in a brown suede jacket and heavy boots, stands over NARRATOR while MA watches blankly from her chair.

(SOUND: television game-show music, Nick’s boot grinding on the floorboards)

NICK

Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday. Where the hell were you?

NARRATOR

I forgot. I was with someone.

NICK

A girl is worth this? Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

NICK slams the apartment door behind him. NARRATOR stands still, then looks toward MA’s pills.

(SOUND: door slam, pill organizer snapping shut)

NARRATOR V.O.
Sometimes I really hated Nick inside.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

NARRATOR struggles on a purple mat among calm students. SIONNA, now in a simple fitted T-shirt and leggings, demonstrates a pose nearby.

(SOUND: slow breathing, floorboards creaking under shifting bodies)

SIONNA

Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

NARRATOR

My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA

That’s why they call it practice.

NARRATOR relaxes flat on the mat, surprised by the stillness.

(MUSIC: a low, sustained singing bowl fading into silence)

NARRATOR V.O.
For once, I did not feel violent, mad, or scared. I just felt calm.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

MA lies in a hospital bed after a blood-pressure crisis. NARRATOR sleeps in a chair. His head falls forward as machines blink beside her.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeping, distant hospital cart wheels)

DREAM - EXT. BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

NARRATOR wears a mud-stained old soldier’s tunic amid fighting men and flashing steel. NICK charges through the smoke carrying a long knife.

(SOUND: swords striking, men crying out, wind roaring)

NARRATOR

Nick! Thank God you’re here!

NICK

That you are, little brother.

NICK drives the blade into NARRATOR’S chest.

(SOUND: a sharp stab, heartbeat pounding, sudden silence)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

NARRATOR bolts awake, gasping. MA dozes peacefully in the bed.

(SOUND: heart monitor resuming its steady beep)

NARRATOR V.O.
Nick had killed me. I kept telling myself it was only a dream.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

SIONNA places a blue weekly pill organizer on the table. MA touches it uncertainly. NICK enters, red-faced, his unruly hair damp from the cold.

(SOUND: keys striking the entry basket, television murmuring)

MA

You’re such good boys. What would I do without you two?

NICK

Man, you shoulda been a nurse.

NARRATOR

Sionna helped me figure it out.

NICK

This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

NARRATOR

Stop it, Nick. You haven’t even met her.

NICK steps close and slaps NARRATOR across the head.

(SOUND: the slap cracking, MA’s frightened inhale)

NICK

Don’t you EVER talk that way about my mother.

NICK storms out.

(SOUND: door slamming, MA’s game show continuing too brightly)

EXT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

NICK, NARRATOR, BOB, and SAMMY enter the noisy bar. Nick spots his former girlfriend LINDSEY with her new boyfriend, CARLOS.

(SOUND: hard rock music, pool balls breaking, crowded bar chatter)

NICK

Who’s she with?

NARRATOR

You broke up with her. Leave it, man.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

NICK closes in on CARLOS at the pool tables. LINDSEY presses herself against the wall. NARRATOR steps between them.

(SOUND: music cutting under a hush, cue sticks tapping against the floor)

NARRATOR

No one cares about your ego, Nick!

NICK draws back his fist and hits NARRATOR. JOE and BOUNCER HARRIS seize NICK as BOB and SAMMY shout.

(SOUND: fist striking flesh, chairs scraping, Lindsey screaming)

LINDSEY

No! Stop!

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

NARRATOR runs through the cold, blood at one nostril. SIONNA opens her door and draws him into the warm living room.

(SOUND: NARRATOR’S ragged breath, wind whipping dead leaves)

SIONNA

Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR

He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick.

SIONNA

No one who really cares about you does that.

NARRATOR lowers himself beside her green suede sofa. She cups his bruised cheek.

NARRATOR

The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA

Me too, honey. Me too.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Months later. NICK’S once-neat apartment is littered with food containers, clothes, and unopened mail. NICK, unshaven and thinner but still imposing, opens the chained door to NARRATOR.

(SOUND: chain lock rattling, microwave humming, traffic below Werner Street)

NICK

You ruined my life. People are saying my little brother tried to kick my ass.

NARRATOR

I didn’t start it. I tried to stop you from hurting somebody.

NICK

You’re not the brother I knew.

NARRATOR

I’m going to school. I’m trying to make something of myself.

NICK

You are my little brother. I’ll let it slide this time.

NICK points a hard finger at NARRATOR.

NICK

Don’t you ever interrupt me in public again.

NARRATOR

Will you see Ma? She’s worried about you.

NICK

Tell her I’ve been sick.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

SIONNA serves peach pie as MA laughs from her chair. NICK arrives in his suede jacket and boots. He sees SIONNA for the first time.

(SOUND: forks scraping plates, the television’s low police-drama dialogue)

SIONNA

I’m Sionna. Nice to meet you.

NICK

Likewise.

NICK studies her with a thin smile.

NICK

I hear you’re a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA

I hope to teach. Education changes when more voices are allowed in it.

NICK

Women oughta stay home and take care of their kids.

SIONNA

Many women have no choice. And diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to live on this planet and make a difference.

MA looks at SIONNA with open admiration. NICK’s smile disappears.

(SOUND: a spoon dropping against a plate, silence from the television)

EXT. WERNER AND LOWE INTERSECTION - NIGHT

NICK’S burnt-orange 1980 Pontiac Trans Am, yellow flames along its side, turns through an intersection. MA sits beside him in her Sunday coat.

(SOUND: engine revving, windshield wipers, distant church bells)

NICK raises a Jack-and-Coke cup from the console. Headlights blast through the driver’s window.

(SOUND: tires screeching, metal crushing, glass exploding)

EXT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

NARRATOR enters the silent apartment. MA’s chair is empty. The phone’s red message light blinks.

(SOUND: answering-machine beep, refrigerator motor, NARRATOR’S uneven breathing)

OFFICER DUPUIS V.O.
There has been a serious accident involving your brother and mother.

OFFICER DUPUIS V.O.
Your mother passed away at the scene. Your brother is in serious condition.

NARRATOR grips the counter until his knuckles whiten.

NARRATOR V.O.
She was dead. And Nick killed her.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

NICK lies bruised and yellowed beneath tubes, his right leg immobilized. NARRATOR stands beside the bed, rigid with grief.

(SOUND: oxygen hiss, monitor alarms, rain ticking on the window)

NICK

I guess I really fucked up, man. Can’t believe Ma’s gone.

NARRATOR

The mass was nice. She’s at peace now.

NICK

They’re saying vehicular manslaughter. I didn’t mean to hurt her.

NARRATOR

Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK glares, trapped by his tubes and bedrails.

(SOUND: monitor quickening, NARRATOR’S footsteps leaving)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

SIONNA prepares dinner in the kitchen. NICK enters unannounced, older, furious, and unsteady on a recovering leg. He blocks SIONNA near the hallway wall.

(SOUND: simmering vegetables, a cat’s startled hiss, rain against the windows)

NICK

You caused all my problems. You got inside my brother’s head.

SIONNA

Nick, this has nothing to do with me.

NICK grips her throat and flicks open a switchblade.

(SOUND: switchblade snapping open, SIONNA struggling for breath)

NICK

Break it off with him. Get me money. Or this goes into your neck.

NARRATOR bursts through the front door and grabs NICK’S arm.

(SOUND: front door slamming, bodies striking the wall)

NICK tears free. The knife cuts into SIONNA’S upper chest. Blood spreads across her shirt as she collapses.

(SOUND: SIONNA’S gasp, knife clattering, NARRATOR shouting)

NARRATOR

Sionna! Stay with me!

NICK flees into the rain.

(SOUND: sirens approaching, rain pounding harder)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Months later. SIONNA, her shoulder scar visible above a loose sweater, sits motionless in a rocking chair. NARRATOR kneels before her.

(SOUND: rocking chair creaking, a clock ticking)

NARRATOR

You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA

I can’t feel me anymore.

NARRATOR

Please stay. We can work it out.

SIONNA

As long as there is doubt, I can’t go on with you.

SIONNA looks down. NARRATOR’s eyes fill, but he does not move.

(MUSIC: a single distant piano note, fading)

NARRATOR V.O.
She had awakened me. Then she was gone.

EXT. PIKE PLACE MARKET - DAY

Seven years later. Adult NARRATOR, now better dressed and more confident, walks Seattle’s wet streets beneath a gray sky. He passes his own storefront: TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

(SOUND: market vendors calling, gulls crying, rain on awnings)

NARRATOR V.O.
I became two people: the child in Wisconsin, and the man in Washington.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles glow around adult NARRATOR on a mat. He breathes through meditation while other students sit quietly.

(SOUND: synchronized breathing, a singing bowl resonating)

INSTRUCTOR MAYA

What are you grateful for?

NARRATOR V.O.
I had a home. Work. A life. But anger still kept me in prison.

DREAM - EXT. GREEN HILLS - DAY

NARRATOR walks beside a serene SOUTH ASIAN SAGE in white robes. A discarded pizza box lies on the road.

(SOUND: wind moving through grass, distant birdsong)

SAGE

Stop judging things. This is not your place.

SAGE

Set your inner sight higher.

NARRATOR wakes in his Seattle apartment beside BOZO, an orange-and-white cat.

(SOUND: cat purring, notebook pages turning)

NARRATOR V.O.
I had to find forgiveness.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

A stark visiting room. NARRATOR sits behind thick plexiglass with a phone. NICK enters in an orange prison uniform, gray at the temples, thinner, his old scars still visible.

(SOUND: locked doors buzzing, phones murmuring, fluorescent lights humming)

NICK

Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR

Hi, Nick. How are you holding up?

NICK

It’s not a cakewalk. I got my routine down.

NARRATOR lifts the receiver tighter to his ear.

NARRATOR

I’m here to tell you I am no longer interested in seeing you or hearing anything you have to say.

NARRATOR

You killed Ma. You tried to kill Sionna. You held me down my whole life.

NICK lowers his eyes.

(SOUND: a guard’s keys jangling, another visitor quietly crying)

NARRATOR

But I forgive you.

NICK looks up, stunned.

NARRATOR

I forgive you because it sets me free. It releases your control over me forever.

NICK’S tears fall onto the gray counter.

NICK

I’m sorry. I’m so sorry.

NICK

You’re more than I ever was. You deserve to live out there.

NARRATOR

I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’re expanding.

NICK

President Olson. That’s fucking amazing.

NARRATOR

My name is Peter Olson.

NICK

I’m proud of you, Peter. I love you.

NARRATOR

I love you too. Get better, Nick.

(SOUND: guard rapping on plexiglass, chairs scraping back)

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

PETER walks toward his departure gate with a small bag. Through wide windows, a plane taxis beneath a bright break in the clouds.

(SOUND: boarding announcements, jet engines building beyond the glass)

PETER V.O.
The weight of thirty years was gone.

PETER V.O.
This has been Nick and me.

FADE OUT.

THE END
